1
00:00:00,480 --> 00:00:03,069
How can a diagram tell you which card to
play?

2
00:00:03,219 --> 00:00:05,518
We’ll get there by starting with seven
points.

3
00:00:05,668 --> 00:00:10,370
In the Fano plane, the triangle’s sides,
its three medians, and the circle are all

4
00:00:10,370 --> 00:00:10,718
lines.

5
00:00:10,868 --> 00:00:13,504
Each contains exactly three marked
points.

6
00:00:17,480 --> 00:00:19,129
Pick points one and four.

7
00:00:19,279 --> 00:00:22,622
Only one line contains both, and it
supplies point two.

8
00:00:22,772 --> 00:00:24,456
Pick four and five instead.

9
00:00:24,606 --> 00:00:27,160
Their line is the circle, and it supplies
six.

10
00:00:27,310 --> 00:00:30,480
The circle works just like any other line
for this incidence rule.

11
00:00:34,480 --> 00:00:37,522
Every pair appears exactly once among the
seven triples.

12
00:00:37,672 --> 00:00:41,097
That is what the notation S of two,
three, seven means.

13
00:00:41,247 --> 00:00:44,231
The first number tells us how many points
we prescribe.

14
00:00:44,381 --> 00:00:46,273
The second gives the block size.

15
00:00:46,423 --> 00:00:49,140
The last gives the size of the entire
point set.

16
00:00:51,480 --> 00:00:56,097
Now ask for six-point blocks on twelve
points, with every five points in exactly

17
00:00:56,097 --> 00:00:56,681
one block.

18
00:00:56,831 --> 00:01:00,752
There are seven hundred ninety-two five-
point subsets, and each block accounts

19
00:01:00,752 --> 00:01:01,557
for six of them.

20
00:01:01,707 --> 00:01:05,735
So the design must contain a hundred
thirty-two blocks, called hexads.

21
00:01:08,480 --> 00:01:12,969
To construct the small Witt design, start
with residues modulo eleven and one

22
00:01:12,969 --> 00:01:14,564
additional point, infinity.

23
00:01:14,714 --> 00:01:17,349
Highlight zero and the five nonzero
squares.

24
00:01:17,499 --> 00:01:21,818
Together they give our seed set, Q, on
this twelve-point projective line.

25
00:01:25,480 --> 00:01:27,442
There are two ways to move the seed.

26
00:01:27,592 --> 00:01:30,599
The first adds one modulo eleven, fixing
infinity.

27
00:01:30,749 --> 00:01:34,859
The second takes the negative reciprocal,
interchanging zero and infinity.

28
00:01:35,009 --> 00:01:39,566
Each acts as a permutation of the twelve
labels, so a six-point set remains a six-

29
00:01:39,566 --> 00:01:40,129
point set.

30
00:01:42,480 --> 00:01:45,127
Apply both permutations to each new set.

31
00:01:45,277 --> 00:01:48,961
Keep a set when it appears for the first
time, and queue it for the same

32
00:01:48,961 --> 00:01:49,480
treatment.

33
00:01:49,630 --> 00:01:52,765
The process stops at a hundred thirty-two
distinct sets.

34
00:01:52,915 --> 00:01:54,586
These are the orbit of the seed.

35
00:01:59,480 --> 00:02:03,776
Now delete one point in each possible way
from every block and count the results.

36
00:02:03,926 --> 00:02:08,289
The verifier finds seven hundred ninety-
two distinct five-point subsets, each

37
00:02:08,289 --> 00:02:09,139
occurring once.

38
00:02:09,289 --> 00:02:13,703
That exhausts the five-point subsets of
twelve points and establishes the Steiner

39
00:02:13,703 --> 00:02:14,200
property.

40
00:02:16,480 --> 00:02:20,451
Curtis’s kitten gives us a geometric way
to recognize those blocks.

41
00:02:20,601 --> 00:02:23,143
The corners are infinity, zero, and one.

42
00:02:23,293 --> 00:02:25,824
The other labels are repeated around the
triangle.

43
00:02:25,974 --> 00:02:30,189
Whenever a label appears twice, the two
appearances represent the same point.

44
00:02:33,480 --> 00:02:37,429
At each corner, arrange the same nine
ordinary points into a three-by-three

45
00:02:37,429 --> 00:02:39,831
affine plane over the field of three
elements.

46
00:02:39,981 --> 00:02:41,722
Rows and columns are lines.

47
00:02:41,872 --> 00:02:46,596
The other two directions wrap around the
grid, as the highlighted modular diagonal

48
00:02:46,596 --> 00:02:46,946
shows.

49
00:02:50,480 --> 00:02:51,885
Choose a row and a column.

50
00:02:52,035 --> 00:02:54,984
Their union has five points because they
share one point.

51
00:02:55,134 --> 00:02:59,697
The two modular diagonal directions also
give perpendicular pairs in this field.

52
00:02:59,847 --> 00:03:01,681
Either kind of union is a cross.

53
00:03:01,831 --> 00:03:03,990
The four points left over form a square.

54
00:03:07,480 --> 00:03:10,092
A line plus all three corners gives a
hexad.

55
00:03:10,242 --> 00:03:11,937
So do two parallel lines.

56
00:03:12,087 --> 00:03:14,131
A cross takes the corner of its own view.

57
00:03:14,281 --> 00:03:16,301
A square takes the other two corners.

58
00:03:16,451 --> 00:03:20,978
The four families have twelve, twelve,
fifty-four, and fifty-four blocks,

59
00:03:20,978 --> 00:03:22,802
exactly the projective orbit.

60
00:03:24,480 --> 00:03:27,975
Conway’s MINIMOG puts the twelve points
into a three-by-four array.

61
00:03:28,125 --> 00:03:30,923
Match the projective and shuffle arrays
cell by cell.

62
00:03:31,073 --> 00:03:33,476
Infinity becomes zero, for example.

63
00:03:33,626 --> 00:03:37,656
The geometry is carried along by this
relabeling, and the new numbers will

64
00:03:37,656 --> 00:03:38,816
serve as card values.

65
00:03:41,480 --> 00:03:45,474
Each tetracode word specifies one row in
each of the four columns.

66
00:03:45,624 --> 00:03:47,423
Choose a and b modulo three.

67
00:03:47,573 --> 00:03:51,416
The row indices are b, a, a plus b, and a
plus twice b.

68
00:03:51,566 --> 00:03:55,537
There are nine words, and any two
coordinates determine the entire word.

69
00:03:58,480 --> 00:04:01,220
In a column with one marked cell, read
the marked row.

70
00:04:01,370 --> 00:04:03,750
With two marked cells, read the missing
row.

71
00:04:03,900 --> 00:04:06,327
Full and empty columns give wildcards.

72
00:04:06,477 --> 00:04:09,135
The specified rows must fit one codeword.

73
00:04:09,285 --> 00:04:13,802
A hand with column counts zero, one, two,
and three is always rejected.

74
00:04:15,480 --> 00:04:17,129
Here is the hand we’ll play.

75
00:04:17,279 --> 00:04:19,798
The columns specify one, one, two, two.

76
00:04:19,948 --> 00:04:22,816
The first two entries force one, one,
two, zero.

77
00:04:22,966 --> 00:04:26,112
The last column disagrees, so the hand is
a nonhexad.

78
00:04:26,262 --> 00:04:28,979
We’ll look for a one-card exchange that
repairs it.

79
00:04:32,480 --> 00:04:34,686
Turn each six-card hand into a vertex.

80
00:04:34,836 --> 00:04:37,646
Join two vertices if their hands share
five cards.

81
00:04:37,796 --> 00:04:40,338
This is the Johnson graph J of twelve,
six.

82
00:04:40,488 --> 00:04:42,717
It has nine hundred twenty-four vertices.

83
00:04:42,867 --> 00:04:47,163
Each hand has thirty-six neighbors, from
six choices out and six choices in.

84
00:04:49,480 --> 00:04:53,598
Two hexads cannot share five cards,
because those five would then have two

85
00:04:53,598 --> 00:04:54,276
completions.

86
00:04:54,426 --> 00:04:56,751
So the hexads form an independent set.

87
00:04:56,901 --> 00:05:01,954
Any independent set uses six distinct
five-subsets per vertex, with no overlap

88
00:05:01,954 --> 00:05:03,069
between vertices.

89
00:05:03,219 --> 00:05:05,680
The resulting upper bound is a hundred
thirty-two.

90
00:05:06,480 --> 00:05:10,991
For a nonhexad, remove any one of its six
cards and complete the five remaining

91
00:05:10,991 --> 00:05:12,610
cards to their unique hexad.

92
00:05:12,760 --> 00:05:14,525
The six completions are distinct.

93
00:05:14,675 --> 00:05:19,063
So every nonhexad has six hexad neighbors
and thirty nonhexad neighbors.

94
00:05:19,213 --> 00:05:22,429
Every hexad has thirty-six nonhexad
neighbors.

95
00:05:23,480 --> 00:05:27,387
The matrix records these counts per
vertex, with rows for the starting class

96
00:05:27,387 --> 00:05:29,366
and columns for the destination class.

97
00:05:29,516 --> 00:05:32,523
For our hand, there are six exchanges to
hexads.

98
00:05:32,673 --> 00:05:34,960
Three increase the total and three
decrease it.

99
00:05:35,110 --> 00:05:36,945
The direction will matter in the game.

100
00:05:40,480 --> 00:05:44,741
Both players see the same six cards, and
the other six are in a shared pile.

101
00:05:44,891 --> 00:05:48,734
On your turn, replace one card with a
smaller card from the pile.

102
00:05:48,884 --> 00:05:50,916
Keep the new total at least twenty-one.

103
00:05:51,066 --> 00:05:53,283
If you have no legal move, you lose.

104
00:05:57,480 --> 00:05:59,082
Our hand totals thirty.

105
00:05:59,232 --> 00:06:03,876
The three decreasing moves to hexads are
four to three, seven to five, and eleven

106
00:06:03,876 --> 00:06:04,341
to nine.

107
00:06:04,491 --> 00:06:07,834
Their new totals are twenty-nine, twenty-
eight, and twenty-eight.

108
00:06:07,984 --> 00:06:10,237
Each leaves the opponent a losing
position.

109
00:06:10,387 --> 00:06:11,908
Let’s play seven to five.

110
00:06:14,480 --> 00:06:17,139
At twenty-eight, Player Two holds a
hexad.

111
00:06:17,289 --> 00:06:21,724
Its odd-man-out pattern extends to the
codeword two, one, zero, two.

112
00:06:21,874 --> 00:06:24,823
In this play, Player Two swaps eleven for
seven.

113
00:06:24,973 --> 00:06:29,281
The total falls to twenty-four, and the
resulting hand has the prohibited column

114
00:06:29,281 --> 00:06:29,663
counts.

115
00:06:31,480 --> 00:06:33,651
Player One replaces six with three.

116
00:06:33,801 --> 00:06:37,946
Now the total is exactly twenty-one, and
the hand is a hexad again.

117
00:06:38,096 --> 00:06:41,486
Every exchange to a smaller card would go
below the threshold.

118
00:06:41,636 --> 00:06:44,817
Player Two has no legal move, so Player
One wins.

119
00:06:48,480 --> 00:06:52,565
Direct every edge toward the smaller
total and keep the vertices at twenty-one

120
00:06:52,565 --> 00:06:53,043
or above.

121
00:06:53,193 --> 00:06:56,606
Totals strictly decrease, so there are no
directed cycles.

122
00:06:56,756 --> 00:06:58,602
Process hands in increasing total.

123
00:06:58,752 --> 00:07:02,525
A hand is losing exactly when none of its
legal successors is losing.

124
00:07:05,480 --> 00:07:10,106
Across all nine hundred five legal hands,
the recursion finds exactly the hundred

125
00:07:10,106 --> 00:07:11,610
thirty-two shuffle hexads.

126
00:07:11,760 --> 00:07:15,708
There are no edges between them, and
every other legal hand has a move into

127
00:07:15,708 --> 00:07:15,975
them.

128
00:07:16,125 --> 00:07:20,815
That makes them the kernel, and explains
why repairing the design wins the game.
